{
  "id": "north_korean_minjok_eumak",
  "name": "Minjok eumak (North Korean reformed national music)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "North Korean national music rebuilt Korean instruments from the 1960s so they could play in equal temperament and in orchestral sections: the haegeum became the four-string sohaegeum and its larger sizes, the taepyeongso the keyed jangsaenap, and new instruments such as the okryugeum zither were devised. Folk-song singing likewise replaced the husky pansori voice with a clear, bright tone, heard in Mansudae Art Troupe recordings. The taepyeongso card stands in for the jangsaenap and the modern extended-range gayageum variant for the reformed North Korean gayageum.",
  "recipe": "classic Asian mid-1960s minjok eumak, art Music, east Asian branch. fast rate, solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. paulownia Korean gayageum, fifths tuned vibrato rich equal tempered section blend sohaegeum, daegeum, reformed dense wood brass belled taepyeongso. treated studio. large-diaphragm condenser, German V72 tube pre, German tube console, broadcast loudness EQ. tape 15ips hall reverb.",
